:name: Flipper Zero (STM32WB55) - EXPERIMENTAL bring-up
:description: Loads a Flipper firmware image onto the hand-written WB55 platform.

# Usage:
#   renode renode/flipper.resc -e "set bin @sdcard/firmware/official/firmware.bin; start"
# or let tools/firmware_manager.py build the command for you.

$name?="Flipper-WB55"
# Path to the firmware. Override with:  set bin @/path/to/firmware.bin
$bin?=@sdcard/firmware/firmware.bin

using sysbus
mach create $name
machine LoadPlatformDescription @renode/stm32wb55.repl

# --- Optional: force clock/flash "ready" bits so early init loops don't spin ---
# These addresses/values are placeholders - fill in from RM0434 during bring-up.
# Example (RCC_CR HSIRDY-style): sysbus Tag <addr> <size> "RCC_CR" 0xFFFFFFFF
# sysbus Tag <0x58000000 0x4> "RCC_CR" 0x0300FB03

# --- Load firmware (raw .bin at flash base; set SP/PC from the vector table) ---
macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadBinary $bin 0x08000000
    cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000
    cpu SP `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x08000000`
    cpu PC `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x08000004`
"""
runMacro $reset

# If you have an ELF instead (built from source), use this in place of the macro:
#   sysbus LoadELF $bin
#   cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000

# --- Once usart1 is a REAL model (UART.STM32F7_USART), open its console: -------
# showAnalyzer sysbus.usart1

echo ""
echo "Loaded '$bin' onto STM32WB55 (experimental). Type 'start' to run,"
echo "'cpu PC' to inspect, 'sysbus' to view the memory map. See RENODE.md."
